<title>GERMANY: Deutsche Telekom H1 ord profit rises.</title>
<headline>Deutsche Telekom H1 ord profit rises.</headline>
<dateline>BONN 1996-09-17</dateline>
<text>
<p>Six months to June 30:</p>
<p>(in millions of marks unless stated)</p>
<p>    Group net profit			   130     vs 700</p>
<p>    Group net profit (U.S. GAAP)	   500     vs n/a</p>
<p>    Group income from ord. activities    2,700   vs 2,200</p>
<p>    Group extraordinary costs		1,850   vs 300</p>
<p>Of which:</p>
<p>Allocation to reserves for personnel 1,750  vs n/a</p>
<p>     Stock emission charges		     100  vs n/a</p>
<p>    Group sales				  29,700  vs 29,300</p>
<p>Of which:</p>
<p>     Hungarian Telephone Co (Matav)	   932  vs n/a</p>
<p>     Telephone services			22,455  vs 25,456</p>
<p>     Service					1,941  vs  1,976</p>
<p>     Mobile communications		    1,731  vs  1,478</p>
<p>     Broadcasting transmission, CTV	 1,621  vs  2,017</p>
<p>     Text and data services		   1,414  vs  1,277</p>
<p>     Publishing, directories		    263  vs    248</p>
<p>     Other activities			     280  vs    105</p>
<p>    Cash flow				    12,400  vs 12,600</p>
<p>    Financial debt (gross)		  105,000  vs n/a</p>
<p>    Financial debt (net)		     98,000  vs n/a</p>
<p>Financial debt (1.1.95 vs 31.12.95) 125,500  vs 110,500</p>
<p>    Workforce				   207,000  vs 220,500</p>
<p>NOTE - Full name of the German telecomunications company is Deutsche Telekom AG.</p>
<p>Telekom chief finance officer Joachim Kroeske attributed the fall in telephone services revenue of 11.8 percent in the first half to uncertainty among customers after rate changes that took effect on January 1, a software glitch that overcharges customers on January 1, and the shutting down of certain services.</p>
<p>-- William Boston, Bonn Newsroom, 49 228 26097150</p>
